Cheats through the libretro API, and one cheat event after a state load

The libretro core's retro_cheat_reset and retro_cheat_set were empty, so a
frontend had no way to the core's Gateway cheat engine but its file
(cheats/<title id>.txt), read once when the game boots. They now reach the
engine directly: retro_cheat_set adds an enabled Gateway cheat made from the
code (its lines "XXXXXXXX YYYYYYYY", separated by newlines), refusing a code
with any invalid line; retro_cheat_reset removes every cheat. The file is not
written: the frontend owns the list.

CheatEngine::Connect also scheduled its event without removing the one a
loaded state's timing queue already holds (System::serialize connects again
after a load): from the first load on, the cheats ran twice as often (Tools/
3ds-bench cheat, Craftus Reloaded: a counting code ran 54 times in 600 frames,
then 107 after each of three loads). It now unschedules the event first, as
its destructor does: 54 after each load.

--- a/src/citra_libretro/citra_libretro.cpp
+++ b/src/citra_libretro/citra_libretro.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,8 @@
 #include "common/logging/filter.h"
 #include "common/settings.h"
 #include "common/string_util.h"
+#include "core/cheats/cheats.h"
+#include "core/cheats/gateway_cheat.h"
 #include "core/core.h"
 #include "core/frontend/applets/default_applets.h"
 #include "core/frontend/image_interface.h"
@@ -747,6 +749,37 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
-void retro_cheat_reset() {}
+// Cheats through the libretro API, into the core's own Gateway engine: the
+// frontend sets each code with retro_cheat_set and clears them all with
+// retro_cheat_reset. A code is the Gateway lines ("XXXXXXXX YYYYYYYY") separated
+// by newlines; one that has no valid line is refused (nothing is added). The
+// engine's cheat file for the title (cheats/<title id>.txt) is not written: the
+// frontend owns the list and sets it again after each load.
+void retro_cheat_reset() {
+    auto& engine = Core::System::GetInstance().CheatEngine();
+    for (std::size_t i = engine.GetCheats().size(); i > 0; i--) {
+        engine.RemoveCheat(i - 1);
+    }
+}
 
-void retro_cheat_set(unsigned index, bool enabled, const char* code) {}
+void retro_cheat_set(unsigned index, bool enabled, const char* code) {
+    if (!code || !enabled) {
+        return;
+    }
+    auto cheat = std::make_shared<Cheats::GatewayCheat>("libretro " + std::to_string(index),
+                                                        std::string(code), "");
+    bool any_valid = false;
+    for (const auto& line : Common::SplitString(cheat->GetCode(), '\n')) {
+        if (Cheats::GatewayCheat::CheatLine(line).valid) {
+            any_valid = true;
+        } else if (!line.empty()) {
+            LOG_ERROR(Frontend, "Cheat {} refused: invalid line {}", index, line);
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+    if (!any_valid) {
+        return;
+    }
+    cheat->SetEnabled(true);
+    Core::System::GetInstance().CheatEngine().AddCheat(std::move(cheat));
+}
--- a/src/core/cheats/cheats.cpp
+++ b/src/core/cheats/cheats.cpp
@@ -30,6 +30,10 @@
     event = system.CoreTiming().RegisterEvent(
         "CheatCore::run_event",
         [this](u64 thread_id, s64 cycle_late) { RunCallback(thread_id, cycle_late); });
+    // After a state load the restored event queue already holds this event
+    // (System::serialize connects again once the queue is unlocked): without
+    // this, every load added a second one and the cheats ran twice as often.
+    system.CoreTiming().UnscheduleEvent(event, 0);
     system.CoreTiming().ScheduleEvent(run_interval_ticks, event);
 }
 
